  "worked_solution": "For this integral, we choose $u=2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+5$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x}.$ Filling in our table gives the following:\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same-color Products** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+5$ | ${e}^{2x}$ |  |  |\n| $6{x}^{2}+6x$ | $\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}$ | $+$ | $\\frac{(2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x}}{2}$ |\n| $12x+6$ | $\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{(3{x}^{2}+3x){e}^{2x}}{2}$ |\n| $12$ | $\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{8}$ | $+$ | $\\frac{(6x+3){e}^{2x}}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{16}$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{3{e}^{2x}}{4}$ |\n\nWe add up all the terms of the last column to get the answer for our integral. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}[2(2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+5)-2(3{x}^{2}+3x)+(6x+3)-3]+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}(4{x}^{3}+10)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}(2{x}^{3}+5)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
